 thank you councillor saunders has moved  does it do i have a second uh  councillors pugh  there are no speakers under 20.1  so do members agree  breathe  [Applause]  item 5  hra budget  to introduce councillor david saunders  thank you again  general item 5 hra budget 2022-23  is covered on pages 19-34  within your agenda pack  next year's hra budget is underpinned by  a 4.1 percent increase in rents next  year the majority of which will be  covered by increased benefit or  universal credit payments to our tenants  this increase  this rent increase is needed to continue  to drive the hra to a balanced and  sustainable financial position and also  to finance our ambassador ambitious  house building program and housing  investment plan  i move the recommendations one to five  on page 19.  thank you councillor saunders and do i  have a second down  counsellor  bob bayford  speaking under rule 20.1 councillor  whitehead  thank you very much leader um my point  is in relation to 0.3.9  and the obvious need for our housing  stock to meet net zero carbon  um as we've identified 12 million pounds  towards the essential capital project  over the next 30 years but we still have  26 million pounds to find do we know the  likelihood of meeting this through the  social housing decarbonisation fund or  do we have an estimation of likely  required borrowing  thank  you  council saunders if you'd like to  respond i'd love to respond  thank you councillor whitehead this is  not my portfolio  i'm interested in the financial side of  the activities so if i may i will defer  to the director of housing and planning  who i hope can put some  light on the situation  thank you council whitehead for the  question um  so we have done some um work on  projecting the potential costs  in the housing revenue account um for  meeting the climate change objectives of  net zero for the housing stock  and you're quite right in the question  that the cost of that um exceeds the  available resources that we currently  have identified in the plan for things  like energy efficiency and heating  upgrades  and  that  that that gap will need to be covered  from um external sources in some way um  the social housing i don't have the  exact figures in front of me i'm afraid  but the social housing decarbonisation  fund so national program funded by  government does have a substantial  amount of money in available for exactly  that purpose  and as we  move forward with this work uh we will  be seeking support from that fund to  help with the costs of meeting those  objectives  at this stage i'm afraid we don't have  the exact figures  but you're absolutely right there is  likely to be a substantial requirement  for external funding  thank you very much for the answer  thank thank you are you okay with that  counsellor saunders um i think we're  moving on to um item six budget 20 22 23  and mid-term financial strategy oh sorry  we haven't actually voted on there on  them  you have to just wait and put my hand up  sorry yes two members agree great  sorry item six budget 2022-23 a  medium-term financial strategy  22-26 to introduce councillor saunders  thank you very much before i introduce  the item once again i'd like to place on  record the help and assistance that has  been given by  chris blundell and his financial team  particularly with regards to the budget  briefings which every member of this  council has had the opportunity of  attending and asking relevant questions  on the evening thank you  general item 6 budget 2022-23  and mtfs  2022-23 covered by pages 35 to 78 in  your agenda pack  i'm very pleased to be able to present a  balanced budget for the 2022-23  to cabinet tonight  just a few months back we were  forecasting a significant budget gap but  with a planned and cautious approach  combined with an efficient savings plan  we have been able to balance our budget  despite what may seem like a lengthy and  complicated process this level of  planning is of critical importance it's  how we continue to deliver the services  that matter to the people and protect  and support our most vulnerable  residents  [Applause]  planning our finances in this way means  we are on the right track for balancing  our budget in the coming years  our balanced budget position has been  supported in part by the very welcome  additional funding that has been  provided by to us by the government the  next year however much of this funding  is temporary and therefore this cabinet  proposes to use these monies in a  financially sound way  for example we plan to use the extra  one-off new homes bonus windfall to pay  off debt and hence putting us on a solid  financial footing as we prepare for the  reform of local government funding and  the uncertainty this brings  despite the additional funding we have  received it has been necessary to find  budget savings in order to balance next  year's budget one of our priorities  throughout the budget setting process  has been to protect jobs as much as  possible and as such a key component of  our budget strategy has been to look for  savings from vacant posts  i am therefore pleased to present  savings  a quarter of a million pounds has been  found from this review  in addition to this we have also found  almost another four hundred thousand  pounds from further savings efficiencies  and income generation opportunities  finally the proposed increase in council  tax at five pounds per band d  equivalent plot property  represents an increase of less than 10  pence a week for each household  i move the recommendations one and two  as detailed on page 35. thank you  thank you councillor saunders council  saunders has moved  would have a second councillor george  cup  speaking under 20.1 i have first  councillor everett  yeah thank you leader and um i'd like to  echo what council saunders has said in  terms of  praising mr blundell and the finance  team for their hard work and  particularly for the briefing that mr  blundell gave to the labor group that  was very helpful and appropriate and  labor group will continue to engage in  the budget process as you'd expect  obviously we welcome the unexpected  additional funding  although i would say that it does point  to the uncertain environment that we  operate in and the difficulty of  actually managing our budget since we  only find out at the last minute how  much money we're going to get  um this is not a new thing as we all  know  um in respect of the new homes bonus  money um while it's particularly welcome  it's not government large yes  it's been triggered  um by the council exceeding the  threshold  of new properties and properties brought  back into use i mean i'm particularly  proud of the number of empty homes  brought back into use by this council i  think it's something that we perform  particularly well at although perhaps we  have more of them in the first place  um somebody would be able to give me  some statistical advice on that i'm sure  i mean the new homes bonus story reminds  me that um this is more or less where i  came in eight years ago when new homes  bonus had just been launched and we were  all exploring what was what it was for  and discussing whether or not it ought  to be built into the base budget  um clearly that's not the case and  unfortunately  um  practically all of this new funding if  not all of it is uh one-off payments so  it's not quite party time in fanec yet  even if it is in downing street  um so this extra money can't be used  unfortunately for doing the things that  our residents would most like which is  to improve  street cleansing and rubbish collection  i appreciate the council has other ideas  about those but there is a limit to the  way we can do it because we can't build  it into the base budget or guarantee  what might be the situation next year  and it seems to me that this uncertainty  which doesn't just extend to annual  funding extensive the whole funding  formula is the biggest challenge that  the council currently faces  and i don't think whoever was in power  will be able to come up with a perfect  solution to that at this time  i'm sure that  through the cycle of meetings over the  next month we will have more to say  about the budget but that's all i wish  to say tonight  thank you councillor everett um would  you like to respond although there  wasn't necessarily a question  much of  what councillor everett said  particularly one-off payments and the  uncertainty i absolutely agree with  and i look forward to further debate  with regards to the budget before we  reach the month of february thank you  next to speak under 20.1 councillor  yates  thank you leader  the labour group have gone through the  budget in detail  and we do believe there's real  opportunity for investment in our public  assets  investment into our housing program and  an opportunity to support officers in  tackling the climate emergency  as mentioned already we'll go through  the details these changes at the  appropriate time  which for us is the review and scrutiny  meeting but we're keen to stress we want  cross-party support for these measures  and believe they do make financial sense  will result in a balanced budget  and do represent the expectations of the  people who've done it thank you  thank you councillor yates you just wish  to respond  i'd like to thank councillor yates and  look forward further debate  as always  speaking under 20.1 councillor  austin thank you leader i'd like to  echo what's been said about the um  success of chris rondell and the finance  team in getting us to this point and the  helpfulness of the budget briefing which  for me as a relatively new council was  particularly useful thank you very much  i just wanted to ask one question which  actually picks up on what various other  speakers have said which was about the  vehicle replacement policy and whether  we're going to take the opportunity in  the light of the climate emergency to  trial  for example an electric waste vehicle  and whether we are considering the  possibility when we're looking at new uh  waste rounds  of  possibly  investing in  at least one smaller vehicle that could  offer recycling services to some of  those streets that don't have curbside  recycling at the moment because the  streets are too narrow for the existing  vehicles to get down i'd be really  interested in whether there are any  possibilities there to extend our work  on on addressing climate change thank  you  thank you council austin i that counts  as soon as you'd probably like to come  back but also i think councillor baifen  can also add to  to commentary on this one yes i welcome  commentary from council bayford uh what  i can say is that cabinet have  considered  electric vehicles electric phrases um  there we don't at the moment have the  infrastructure  to seriously consider it in the in the  short term it's a long-term objective at  the moment i am advised that the  electric freighters although  you'll see buses and coaches which are  working quite well in taxes  the issue with the elect with the having  electric freighters if that is the  lifting gear on the rear  absorbs the battery power very very  rapidly and we probably have to invest  in more freighters than we currently  have in order the phrases can come back  to the depot if we had the  infrastructure at the depot to charge  them in order to get recharged but it is  something that we've been looking at  seriously and considering um but uh for  the purposes of this budget um i'm  afraid it's it's something that um it's  on our wish list but we can't actually  fulfill that wish within the next  financial year thank you council  bathroom yeah i mean i think  council someone has actually said a lot  of what i would say um but there you go  no i mean it's something that we're very  conscious of something that we'd love to  do but you're probably aware at the  moment that elec